WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.820
Building an AI demo takes a day.

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.163
Taking it to production takes months.

3
00:00:05.543 --> 00:00:11.217
In this video, we look at why, and at the architecture AI App Server uses to close that gap.

4
00:00:12.297 --> 00:00:14.985
The model call itself is a few lines of code.

5
00:00:15.365 --> 00:00:21.424
Around it, a production app needs a vector database and an ingestion pipeline, memory and a cache,

6
00:00:21.804 --> 00:00:24.833
a job queue, and a gateway across model providers.

7
00:00:25.213 --> 00:00:29.245
Then guardrails, sign-in and audit, deploys, monitoring and backups.

8
00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:34.148
That's eight systems to choose, secure, pay for and run, for every single app.

9
00:00:35.228 --> 00:00:38.236
AI App Server is an application server for AI.

10
00:00:38.616 --> 00:00:45.763
You deploy chat, document extraction, agents and workflows the way you deploy web apps, on your own infrastructure.

11
00:00:46.143 --> 00:00:48.916
Let's walk through the five design choices behind it.

12
00:00:49.996 --> 00:00:51.447
First: one binary.

13
00:00:51.827 --> 00:00:59.997
Document search is built in: hybrid search over PDFs, Office files, web pages, and connectors such as SharePoint and Git.

14
00:01:00.377 --> 00:01:06.543
So are key-value memory, a cache, background jobs and event topics, with retries and dead letters.

15
00:01:06.923 --> 00:01:15.456
Every model call goes through a built-in gateway, to OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Bedrock, or a local model.

16
00:01:15.836 --> 00:01:24.177
And guardrails, sign-in, encryption and audit come with it, so you install one small download instead of wiring eight products together.

17
00:01:25.257 --> 00:01:26.879
Here is how a request flows.

18
00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:31.632
A browser, an app or an agent calls the server over HTTPS.

19
00:01:32.012 --> 00:01:33.953
Guardrails check the request first.

20
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:37.811
Then your app's workers handle it, in Python or JavaScript.

21
00:01:38.191 --> 00:01:45.615
Workers call models through the gateway, which counts tokens and cost, enforces budgets, caches, retries, and fails over.

22
00:01:45.995 --> 00:01:53.077
And everything the app stores, from documents and vectors to memory, jobs and events, is encrypted on the server.

23
00:01:54.157 --> 00:01:58.424
An app is just a folder, with one config file: ai-app.yaml.

24
00:01:58.804 --> 00:02:03.092
This one is a complete chat over documents, from the knowledge-chat blueprint.

25
00:02:03.472 --> 00:02:10.469
It names its models, here two local models through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, and the documents to search.

26
00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:15.927
It turns on guardrails that redact personal data and refuse prompt-injection attempts.

27
00:02:16.307 --> 00:02:22.216
Every setting is commented, and you add Python or JavaScript only where you need custom behaviour.

28
00:02:23.296 --> 00:02:26.880
Here is a real run, against a local model on a CPU.

29
00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.607
aiapp new creates the app from a blueprint.

30
00:02:29.987 --> 00:02:32.056
source add points it at your documents.

31
00:02:32.436 --> 00:02:37.087
aiapp deploy indexes them and starts the app, with a web page and an API.

32
00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:42.480
Ask a question, and the answer streams back with a citation to the document it came from.

33
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.445
Second: enterprise security is part of the platform, not something each app adds.

34
00:02:48.825 --> 00:02:53.199
Every app gets the same sign-in, and everything the server stores is encrypted.

35
00:02:53.579 --> 00:02:59.936
Every model call and every guardrail decision is audited, and logs never contain prompts or answers.

36
00:03:00.316 --> 00:03:03.153
Part two of this series covers security in detail.

37
00:03:04.233 --> 00:03:05.492
Third: clusters.

38
00:03:05.872 --> 00:03:10.523
Several servers form a cluster using Raft, a proven consensus protocol.

39
00:03:10.903 --> 00:03:14.679
Every node holds all the data, and every node serves requests.

40
00:03:15.059 --> 00:03:23.037
A write is acknowledged only after a majority of nodes have stored it, so a three-node cluster can lose any one node without losing data.

41
00:03:23.417 --> 00:03:26.895
If the leader fails, another node is elected in about a second.

42
00:03:27.275 --> 00:03:34.336
In our node-loss test, on three containers on one machine, about twenty-six hundred writes went through, and none were lost.

43
00:03:35.416 --> 00:03:39.448
Fourth: open protocols, so the code and tools you have keep working.

44
00:03:39.828 --> 00:03:47.167
Chat apps answer the OpenAI chat API, so any OpenAI client can call them by changing the base URL.

45
00:03:47.547 --> 00:03:55.717
Apps can publish tools to AI agents over the Model Context Protocol, MCP, and call MCP servers themselves.

46
00:03:56.097 --> 00:04:01.409
Redis clients, such as redis-cli and redis-py, can read and write an app's data.

47
00:04:01.789 --> 00:04:06.696
And topics in Kafka or Azure Event Hubs flow in and out of apps through bridges.

48
00:04:07.776 --> 00:04:09.739
Fifth: isolation between apps.

49
00:04:10.119 --> 00:04:17.159
Each app runs in its own worker processes, with memory and file-size limits, and can't read another app's data.

50
00:04:17.539 --> 00:04:21.699
Quotas cap storage, vectors, concurrent requests and worker memory,

51
00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:26.239
and model spend per day or per month, for the app and for each API key.

52
00:04:26.619 --> 00:04:31.973
So one misbehaving app can't exhaust another app's memory, disk, budget or workers.

53
00:04:32.353 --> 00:04:37.601
We tested exactly that on Linux: a misbehaving app running next to a well-behaved one.

54
00:04:38.681 --> 00:04:46.191
So that's the architecture: one binary, enterprise security, Raft clusters, open protocols, and per-app isolation.

55
00:04:47.271 --> 00:04:52.924
Next in this series: enterprise security, and how one review of the platform covers every app.

56
00:04:53.304 --> 00:05:00.259
AI App Server is free to use, and its SDKs, example apps and templates are Apache 2.0.

57
00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:07.167
Download it and follow the ten-minute quickstart at github.com/Sandaal/ai-app-server.

